  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 408 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nQuiet conditions across Minnesota & western Wisconsin \novernight, despite two areas of thunderstorms ongoing across \nthe eastern Dakotas. These clusters of thunderstorms will wane \ninto the morning as they move eastwards, with perhaps some \nlingering light rain across southwest Minnesota through mid to \nlate morning. Temperatures will again be around 80 degrees this \nafternoon, except across southern Minnesota where cloud cover \nwill keep temperatures a few degrees cooler. Dew points increase\nthrough the day, especially across western Minnesota, leading \nmore muggy conditions compared to over the weekend.\n\nA shortwave passes over the region tonight into Tuesday with a\ncold front passing through the region through the day on\nTuesday. Overnight, a cluster of thunderstorms is expected to\ndevelop tonight across eastern North Dakota. These storms could\ninitially contain large hail & damaging winds, but are \nexpected to weaken as they move eastwards into Minnesota after \nmidnight. High resolution models are still varied on just how \nlong these thunderstorms may hold together overnight into \nTuesday morning, with the best chances for any rain expected \nacross west-central Minnesota. Cloud cover from these lingering \nshowers & thunderstorms could linger into the afternoon, \npotentially limiting the instability present for a potential \nsecond round of storms Tuesday late Tuesday afternoon & \nevening. Recent guidance has generally slowed down the passage \nof the cold front, which could give far-eastern Minnesota & \nwestern Wisconsin more time to destabilize Tuesday afternoon. \nDew points in the low to mid 60s combined with temperatures \nwarming into the 80s could support around 1000-1500 J/kg of \nCAPE, while 30-40 kts of deep shear will be more than enough for\nstorms to organize. If we can realize this environment, \nscattered strong to severe thunderstorms would likely develop \nalong the front during the afternoon & move eastwards through \nthe evening, with damaging winds & large hail the main threats. \nSo while confidence is increasing that at least a few strong to \nsevere storms will be possible Tuesday afternoon & evening, the \nmagnitude & coverage of the threat still depends heavily on how \nquickly the atmosphere can recover during the day.\n\nThe cold front exits into Wisconsin Tuesday night with cooler & drier\nnorthwest flow returning Wednesday. A few showers could linger \nWednesday, mainly closer to the upper low across northern \nMinnesota & Wisconsin, but most of our area should trend dry \nthrough the day. High pressure then brings quiet weather \nThursday into Friday with highs generally around 80 & \ncomfortable humidity. The pattern looks to become more active \nagain heading into next weekend as multiple shortwaves move \nthrough the northern Plains & Upper Midwest. This should bring \nincreasing chances for showers & thunderstorms sometime Friday \nnight through Sunday, but timing differences within the guidance\nlead to low confidence in the details this far out.",
